	abstract = {This study examines how fiscal decentralization affects urban-rural inequality in income acquisition opportunities in China. Using microdata from the China Family Panel Studies (CFPS), we construct a counterfactual model to isolate income disparities driven solely by urban-rural structural differences. The results show that fiscal decentralization significantly exacerbates such inequality, primarily by inducing urban-biased public resource allocation as a result of promotion-driven competition among local governments. The effect is more pronounced in cities with intense intergovernmental competition and lower agricultural dependence, while it is attenuated in cities with higher levels of urban-rural integration and greater reliance on intergovernmental transfers. This paper expands the analytical perspective of equality of opportunity and offers a novel approach to understanding the root causes of urban-rural income disparities. © 2025 Elsevier Inc.},
