	abstract = {The fiscal behavior of local governemnts has gained significant attention in academic and policymaking circles over the last decades, as the result of the proliferation of fiscal decentralization reforms. Against this background, muncipal budgets cannot anymore be considered as merely technical instruments of financial management, but they have to be conceptualized and studied, as potentially contested political arenas, at the local scale, on which economic, social and political communities of actors compete for access to public goods and services. In this paper we investigate the effects of the introduction of a strict municipal balanced-budget fiscal rule in 2014 in Flanders, Belgium, with respect to the reshaping of local governments' expenditure structure. We do so with a dynamic spatial Durbin model on a unique panel data (2006–2021), with which we are able to capture both temporal persistence and spatial spillovers in spending patterns. The empirical results show that the introduction of the fiscal rule not only changed the expenditure priorities of local governments and lowered fiscal discretion and flexibility, but also decreased inter-municipal policy interdependence, especially in policy areas such as culture, environment, and public safety. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd.},
