	abstract = {This paper analyses the introduction of the Poll Tax in the U.K. in April 1990 within the context of government failure in tax reform. Two views of government failure are considered. The failure may be 'benign', where the government is attempting to maximise social welfare but fails to do so because imperfections in the political process through which the public interest is revealed provide the wrong information regarding socially optimal policies. Alternatively, 'Leviathan' failure is 'malign' and occurs when the government ignores the public interest in order to pursue its own objectives. An analysis of the Poll Tax illustrates the difficulties associated with determining which form of government failure is present. As the reform appeared to be structured so as to increase local accountability, especially by reducing fiscal illusion, any failure could have been benign. On the other hand, the reform was consistent with a rent seeking strategy on the part of central government. We analyse the reform by reference to criteria of efficiency and equity in an attempt to differentiate between alternative forms of government failure. However it is when the tax reform is analysed using variations on a simple Downsian politico-economic model that greatest doubt is cast on the argument that failure was simply benign. © 1993.},
