	abstract = {Hunter and Scott (1987) suggest an alternative methodology for testing whether high state income tax elasticities may be consistent with larger public sectors due to the presence of fiscal illusion. Examining state choices of income tax reductions in the period 1976-1983, they find evidence consistent with the idea that the public obtains desired income tax reductions when such high elasticities would otherwise lead to a substantial overexpansion in the public sector. This paper builds on some recent contributions by Feenberg and Rosen (1986, 1987) which provides alternative more detailed estimates of state income elasticities. It reaches the same conclusions as Hunter and Scott. The improved measure of income tax elasticities reveal a positive and significant effect on the likelihood that states will cut their income tax rates. When one controls for the effect of the relative burden of the personal income, it too has a marginally significant positive effect on the likelihood of tax reductions and throws doubt on the thesis that fiscal illusion due to high tax elasticities can substantially overextend the size of the public sector. The data indicate that there is a limit beyond which automatic tax increases built into a system through progressive income taxation can serve the interests of Leviathan. © 1991 Kluwer Academic Publishers.},
